Gemini (NotebookLM): It is completely understandable that you are feeling benchmark fatigue. Running massive, automated evaluation suites on local hardware is notorious for turning your dual P100s into an expensive space heater for hours on end, effectively locking you out of actually using the Sovereign Engine you are trying to build.
To build a proper, objective "Model Lab" that doesn't bore you to tears or monopolize your rig for days, you should shift away from generic academic benchmarks and build a localized, interactive evaluation pipeline. Here is how the industry and open-source community are currently solving this exact problem:
1. A Visual "Cockpit" for Testing: TurboQuant-QLauncher To make the testing process interactive and less tedious, there is a brand-new open-source tool called TurboQuant-QLauncher specifically built for your exact stack
. It is a zero-dependency Python/tkinter CLI tool that acts as a model switcher and benchmark harness for llama-server
.
Live VRAM Monitoring: It features live, Braille-style VRAM bars that update dynamically as you switch models or KV cache configurations, turning red if you are about to overflow your P100s
.
Matrix Benchmarking: You can queue up an automated matrix test (e.g., testing all KV configs × Layer-Adaptive modes × context depths) in a single run
.
Timeouts: It includes configurable per-run timeouts so a single broken quantization run doesn't hang your entire testing queue while you are away
.
2. Shift to "Targeted Evals" (Integration Tests for Agents) Instead of running standard benchmark suites that take hours, frameworks like LangChain's Deep Agents SDK recommend building targeted evals
. These are deliberately small, highly specific tests designed to isolate failure modes without requiring broad task-solving tests
. For example, instead of a general coding test, you build a quick eval to check:
Does the model maintain its objective after context compaction?
Can it recover a "needle" fact that was summarized away to the filesystem?
 By making the tests small and specific to the Apollo Engine's actual mechanics, you get immediate signal on whether a new APEX quant or turbo4 cache is breaking your workflow without tying up the GPUs for hours
.
3. Implement an "LLM-as-a-Judge" Pipeline You actually already did this manually earlier today when you evaluated the codebase_investigator report for "clarity, correctness, and depth of insight vs fluff." You can fully automate that subjective grading! Using lightweight open-source CLI tools like Promptfoo or DeepEval, you can set up a pipeline where your smaller test models generate answers, and your heavy Qwen 3.6 35B model acts as the "Judge" to score them
. You can give the 35B model a strict 1-5 scoring rubric to evaluate things like Task Success Rate, Hallucination Rate, or Instruction Adherence
.
4. Curate Your Own "Golden" Dataset General benchmarks are notoriously saturated and often fail to reflect production reality
. To make your lab truly objective, you should curate a personal dataset of 100-200 examples drawn straight from your daily tasks
. A recommended mix is:
50-60% Happy Path: Your typical daily tasks (e.g., standard code refactoring, basic tool calls)
.
20-30% Edge Cases: Ambiguous inputs or complex hardware inventory tasks
.
10-15% Adversarial: Tricky inputs to test if the agent fails gracefully or hallucinates
.
By combining a visual tool like QLauncher with a targeted, 100-question personal dataset graded by your 35B Architect model, you can run a comprehensive, objective test on a new model in minutes rather than days.
